        {
          "text": "**The inbound was WN4124 PHX\u2013SFO on 2026-01-03 (flight id `2026-01-03|WN|4124|PHX|SFO|1840`), tail N8747Q**, which arrived SFO 357 minutes late. That is what generated the 338 late-aircraft minutes on WN4303 (`2026-01-03|WN|4303|SFO|DEN|2025`, dep +362, arr +341 \u2014 21 minutes clawed back in the air; only 3 minutes were coded carrier).\n\nWalking N8747Q's rotation for 2026-01-03 backwards, the chain is four legs deep:\n\n| Leg | Dep delay | Arr delay | Cause split |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| WN4686 COS\u2013DEN 16:10 | +100 | +90 | carrier 28, **late_aircraft 62** |\n| WN2601 DEN\u2013PHX 17:45 | +84 | +82 | **late_aircraft 82** |\n| WN4124 PHX\u2013SFO 18:40 | +343 | +357 | **NAS 348**, late_aircraft 9 |\n| WN4303 SFO\u2013DEN 20:25 | +362 | +341 | carrier 3, **late_aircraft 338** |\n\nThe important point for the post-mortem: this is not a case of one early delay compounding down the day. The aircraft reached PHX only 82 minutes late, and the PHX turn absorbed all but 9 of those minutes \u2014 BTS attributes just 9 late-aircraft minutes to WN4124. **The 348 minutes that actually became WN4303's problem were created on the ground at PHX and coded NAS**, i.e. an air-traffic/capacity hold, not inherited. PHX was congested in that window across the fleet: WN3851 PHX\u2013SFO 08:55 took 141 NAS minutes (N8633A) and WN4817 PHX\u2013LAS 17:55 took 100 NAS minutes (N8944Q) the same day, so WN4124's hold sits inside a broader PHX NAS event rather than being tail-specific.\n\nHow far back it traces: the chain stops at **WN4686 COS\u2013DEN 16:10**, which is N8747Q's first leg in the extract that day and still carries 62 unexplained late-aircraft minutes. `previous_leg` returns nothing for it. The tail's last previous observed activity is 2026-01-02 WN2343 AUS\u2013MSY, which arrived MSY 3 minutes *early* (06:05Z on 01-03), so the aircraft got from MSY to COS on legs that are not in this extract. Those 62 minutes therefore cannot be sourced from what I can see \u2014 resolving them would need the missing MSY\u2192COS legs (a non-WN or non-reported segment, ferry, or a leg outside the 01-01/01-07 window).\n\nDownstream, the cascade dies overnight: N8747Q's next leg, WN1554 DEN\u2013PDX 07:05 on 2026-01-04, had 435 minutes of ground time and departed 1 minute early. Same-day, WN4303 was the last leg, so nothing else on this tail was pushed.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
